## Required attribution

To comply with the Attribution condition, credit the material like this, substituting the actual repository URL and adapting the wording to your medium:

> Based on content from **CyberMastery** (<https://github.com/>*`<user>`*/*`<repo>`*), licensed under [CC BY-NC-SA 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/). Changes were made.

That credit contains the three things the condition requires:

1. **A credit to CyberMastery**, by name.
2. **A link to the repository**, so a reader can find the original. Replace the `<user>` and `<repo>` placeholders with the owner's GitHub account and repository name. Those two placeholders are the only ones this project permits, and they appear in the credit above precisely because only the owner can fill them in.
3. **A link to the licence**, at <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/>.

**Add a note if changes were made.** If you adapted the material rather than reproducing it, include the sentence "Changes were made." If you reproduced it unchanged, replace that sentence with a statement that it is unmodified. Saying nothing at all leaves a reader unable to tell whether they are looking at CyberMastery's words or yours, which fails the condition.

Where to put the credit depends on the medium: in the footer of a web page, in the front matter or bibliography of a document, in the description of a video, or in a dedicated acknowledgements section. What matters is that it is easy to find and that it is attached to the material rather than buried somewhere unrelated.

