## A practical note on classrooms and commercial reuse

**A teacher may use and adapt this material in a non-commercial classroom with attribution and share-alike.** Concretely: you may print a topic write-up as a handout, put an exercise batch in front of your students, adapt a scenario to your own organisation's environment, translate the material, or build a course unit around it. You must credit CyberMastery with a link to the repository and a link to the licence, note that you made changes if you did, and release your adapted version under the same CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 licence so your students and colleagues have the same freedoms you had.

The NonCommercial condition does not prevent a salaried teacher from using this material in a class they are paid to teach, and it does not prevent an internal corporate security team from using it to train its own staff. In both cases the material is being used to educate, not being sold, and it is not the product.

**Commercial reuse is not permitted.** Bundling this material into a paid course is not permitted. Selling access to it, putting it behind a subscription paywall, packaging it as a paid study guide, or using it as the value proposition of a commercial product is not permitted. Neither is publishing it on an advertising-funded site whose revenue depends on the traffic the material attracts. If you want to do any of those things, you need a separate licence from the copyright holders, and you should ask before assuming one is available.

There is one narrow case worth naming because it is genuinely confusing: because ShareAlike permits distribution of an adaptation at cost, you may charge a fee that recovers the cost of distributing a ShareAlike adaptation, provided the material itself remains available under this licence and the fee is not a commercial profit on the content. Charging tuition for a course whose value *is* this material is a commercial use. Recovering the cost of printing a handout is not. When the distinction is unclear in your situation, ask.
